INT. APARTMENT - KITCHEN - DAWN

A small kitchen. Linoleum. A kettle on the stove, steam rising. Two mugs on the counter, one chipped. A coat hangs on the back of the door, a man's coat, gray wool.

BERNADETTE OYELARAN-STACK, 60s, in a bathrobe, stands at the sink with her hands under the tap. The water runs a long time. She doesn't wash anything.

The phone on the wall rings. Once. Twice. Three times. She lets it. Finally she dries her hands on the robe, reaches over, and picks it up.

                    BERNADETTE
Hello.

                    NURSE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Mrs. Stack? This is Carol, from the hospice.

                    BERNADETTE
Yes.

                    NURSE (V.O.)
(over phone)
I'm so sorry to call so early.

                    BERNADETTE
That's all right.

                    NURSE (V.O.)
(over phone)
He passed about an hour ago. Very peacefully.

Bernadette nods. Looks at the coat on the door. Looks at the two mugs.

                    BERNADETTE
Thank you for telling me.

                    NURSE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Would you like us to send someone?

                    BERNADETTE
No. Not yet.

                    NURSE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Is there anyone with you?

                    BERNADETTE
I'm fine.

She hangs up. Stands very still. The kettle begins to whistle, thin and rising.

She turns off the burner. Takes the chipped mug from the counter. Pours. The tea steams. She sets it in front of the empty chair at the table and sits across from it.

The whistle fades. She wraps both hands around the other mug and holds it close.
